print(f"F1-score:  {f1:.4f}\n")
print(classification_report(y_test, y_pred, target_names=["Stayed (0)", "Churned (1)"]))

cm = confusion_matrix(y_test, y_pred)
tn, fp, fn, tp = cm.ravel()

plt.figure(figsize=(5, 4))
sns.heatmap(cm, annot=True, fmt="d", cmap="Blues",
            xticklabels=["Predicted Stay", "Predicted Churn"],
            yticklabels=["Actually Stayed", "Actually Churned"])
plt.title("Confusion Matrix")
plt.ylabel("Actual")
plt.xlabel("Predicted")
plt.show()

print(f"True Negatives  (TN): {tn}  -> predicted stay, actually stayed")
print(f"False Positives (FP): {fp}  -> predicted churn, actually stayed")
print(f"False Negatives (FN): {fn}  -> predicted stay, actually churned")
print(f"True Positives  (TP): {tp}  -> predicted churn, actually churned\n")

print(f"ACCURACY ({acc:.1%}): Out of {len(y_test):,} test customers, the model classified {tn+tp:,} correctly.")
print(f"  Note: a model that always predicts 'stay' would score {(y_test==0).mean():.1%}, so accuracy alone is misleading here.\n")
print(f"PRECISION ({prec:.1%}): When the model predicts a customer will churn, it is right {prec:.1%} of the time ({tp} of {tp+fp} churn predictions).\n")
print(f"RECALL ({rec:.1%}): Of the {tp+fn} customers who really churned, the model caught {tp} ({rec:.1%}) and missed {fn}.\n")
print(f"F1-SCORE ({f1:.3f}): The harmonic mean of precision and recall; it balances both in a single number.")

md(r"""
**Explanation of the confusion matrix**
- **True Negatives (TN):** customers who stayed and the model correctly predicted they would stay.
- **True Positives (TP):** customers who churned and the model correctly flagged them.
- **False Positives (FP):** customers who stayed but the model predicted they would churn. The company might spend a retention discount on someone who was never leaving.
- **False Negatives (FN):** customers who churned but the model predicted they would stay. These are lost customers the company never had a chance to save.

**Which metric is most important for this problem?**  
**Recall** is the most important metric. The purpose of a churn model is to find customers who are about to leave so the company can try to keep them. A false negative (missing a churner) costs the company that customer's future revenue, while a false positive only costs a small retention offer given to someone who would have stayed anyway. Since missing a churner is more expensive than a false alarm, we want to catch as many true churners as possible. Precision still matters so retention budget isn't wasted, which is why the F1-score is a useful companion metric, but recall is the priority. Accuracy is the least informative here because about 73% of customers don't churn, so a trivial model would already look "accurate."
""")

